      </div></button><code><pre>type Reducer = (accumulator any, value any)=>(nextAccumulator Promise|any)

type Transducer = Reducer=>Reducer</pre></code></div></div><p><span>A reducer is a function that defines a relationship between an accumulator and an item in a transformation, and can be used in a reducing operation, such as with Rubico's </span><a href="/docs/reduce"><span>reduce</span></a><span> or vanilla JavaScript </span><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/reduce"><span>Array.prototype.reduce</span></a><span>.</span></p><p><span>A transducer is a function that takes a reducer and returns another reducer. Transducers enable function chains with reducers - pass a reducer to a transducer to create a reducer with chained functionality. Imagine dominos falling over.</span></p><p><div class="image"><img src="/assets/dominoes.png" alt="dominoes.png"></img></div></p><p><span>It's a good exercise to implement transducers on your own, however Rubico offers production-ready transducers via its </span><a href="/docs/Transducer"><span>Transducer</span></a><span> module.</span></p><p><span>A transducer must be used with a reduce function such as Array </span><code>.reduce</code><span>. Rubico provides async-capable reduce functions as the </span><a href="/docs/transform"><span>transform</span></a><span> and </span><a href="/docs/reduce"><span>reduce</span></a><span> operators.</span></p><p><span>The following example shows the function pipeline </span><code>squaredOdds</code><span> used as a transducer.</span></p><div class="code-runner"><div></div><div style="height: .5em;"></div><div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 36px 26px 1fr;"><button style="text-align: center; border-radius: 2px; width: 36px; padding: 0; cursor: pointer; height: 2em; outline: none; box-shadow: 1px 1px grey;">run</button><span style="visibility: hidden; color: #3f72fc; font-size: .80em; font-weight: 625; position: relative; right: -0.75em; bottom: -0.65em;"> ></span><iframe style="visibility: hidden; height: 6em; position: relative; bottom: -0.05em; width: 99%;"></iframe></div></div><p><span>With Rubico's transducers, it is possible to transform asynchronous sources such as async generators.</span></p><div class="code-runner"><div></div><div style="height: .5em;"></div><div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 36px 26px 1fr;"><button style="text-align: center; border-radius: 2px; width: 36px; padding: 0; cursor: pointer; height: 2em; outline: none; box-shadow: 1px 1px grey;">run</button><span style="visibility: hidden; color: #3f72fc; font-size: .80em; font-weight: 625; position: relative; right: -0.75em; bottom: -0.65em;"> ></span><iframe style="visibility: hidden; height: 6em; position: relative; bottom: -0.05em; width: 99%;"></iframe></div></div><p><span>The above is equivalent to the below with vanilla JavaScript </span><code>for await</code><span>:</span></p><div class="code-runner"><div></div><div style="height: .5em;"></div><div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 36px 26px 1fr;"><button style="text-align: center; border-radius: 2px; width: 36px; padding: 0; cursor: pointer; height: 2em; outline: none; box-shadow: 1px 1px grey;">run</button><span style="visibility: hidden; color: #3f72fc; font-size: .80em; font-weight: 625; position: relative; right: -0.75em; bottom: -0.65em;"> ></span><iframe style="visibility: hidden; height: 6em; position: relative; bottom: -0.05em; width: 99%;"></iframe></div></div><p><span>Rubico's transducers are simple and useful for creating memory efficient data transformations. Get started with transducers at the </span><a href="/docs/Transducer"><span>docs</span></a><span>.</span></p><p><span>Further reading:</span></p><ul><li><p><a href="https://tgvashworth.com/2014/08/31/csp-and-transducers.html"><span>CSP and transducers in JavaScript</span></a></p></li></ul></article></div></div><div class="blog-post inactive"><div class="title"><a href="/blog/a-synchronous-functional-programming-intro" class=""><h1>[A]synchronous Functional Programming